[s0l0!-header][h1]Pointers to Unions[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

A pointer to a union points to the memory location allocated to the union.
A [b]union pointer[/b] is declared by using the keyword [b]union [/b]and the union tag along with * and the pointer name. 
For example, consider the following statements:[code format="c" codeId="2112"]union val {
  int int_num;
  float fl_num;
  char str[20]; 
};

[b]union val info;[/b]
union val *ptr = NULL;
ptr = &info;
ptr->int_num = 10;
printf("info.int_num is %d", info.int_num); [/code]
When you want to access the union members through a pointer, the [b]-> operator[/b] is required.
[note](*ptr).int_num is the same as ptr->int_num.[/note]

