[s0l0!-header][h1]Array of Unions[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

An array can store elements of any data type, including [b]unions[/b]. 
With unions, it is important to keep in mind that only one member of the union can store data for each array element.

After declaring an array of unions, an element is [b]accessible with the index number[/b]. The dot operator is then used to access members of the union, as in the program:[code format="c" codeId="2114"]union val {
  int int_num;
  float fl_num;
  char str[20]; 
};

[b]union val nums[10];[/b]
int k;

for (k = 0; k < 10; k++) {
  nums[k].int_num = k;
}

for (k = 0; k < 10; k++) {
  printf("%d  ", nums[k].int_num);
} [/code]
An array is a data structure that stores collection values that are all [b]the same type[/b]. Arrays of unions allow storing values of [b]different types[/b]. 
[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header][code format="c" codeId="2115"]union type {
  int i_val;
  float f_val;
  char ch_val;
};
union type arr[3];
arr[0].i_val = 42;
arr[1].f_val = 3.14;
arr[2].ch_val = 'x'; [/code]
